From bf6d8d9dd5df05f48f53da92f9f9ff1c96d1b8cd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Day <me@samcday.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 18:29:03 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] adopt: manage declared gadgets in place

A gadget declared with [gadget.<dir>] adopt = true, bound to a UDC, with
one configuration (or config naming one) and every pinned function
present, linked and ready, is adopted instead of only protected: at
startup, before the default mode, and again before every mode switch.
Adopting reads configfs and writes nothing. The mode is read from the
functions linked next to the pins, and tethering is told apart from
developer mode by the state file of the last switch.

On an adopted gadget, charging_only, developer_mode and tethering_mode
run as a transaction: prepare while bound (create function directories,
force-eject every LUN of a leaving mass storage function, stop the old
service, open what the window needs), run the critical window, then
finish while bound again (remove the functions that left, commit, start
the new service). A window that did not reach the target restores the
previous composition or the pins alone, reports the cause, and restarts
the service of the mode the gadget is actually in; if the window thread
is gone, prepare is undone and the old service restarted. If the
composition does not change, as with a pre-composed developer_mode at
boot or developer <-> tethering, nothing in configfs is written. Every
other mode, including host_mode, is refused with "<mode> is not
supported while gadget <g> is adopted" before anything is touched, and
no USB role is written for an adopted gadget. SIGTERM, stop and restart
write nothing to a declared or adopted gadget; the next instance adopts
it again.

A host role excursion removes the UDC, but the gadget keeps its UDC
attribute and the kernel binds it again when the UDC returns. Such a
gadget stays adopted: set_mode refuses to switch it until it is bound
again, and shutdown leaves it alone. Any other gadget usb-signaller must
not replace that waits for its UDC this way makes set_mode fail before
a USB role is written.

The first adoption locks all memory with mlockall(MCL_CURRENT |
MCL_FUTURE) for the rest of the process's life, since a page fault while
the root filesystem is unreachable would never complete. If that fails
the gadget stays protected, not adopted. The tokio runtime is built
explicitly with two workers, four blocking threads and 1 MiB stacks, so
the locked footprint stays small.

/run/usb-signaller/state.env records the gadget, configuration, mode,
NCM interface and connection name after every switch of an adopted
gadget, written atomically and shell-quoted. It is removed before
usb-signaller switches one of its own gadgets, so their services never
read a stale one. The developer and tethering scripts read it and fall
back to their previous lookup.

usb-signaller --plan <mode> prints what a switch would do on every UDC,
from live configfs, without writing anything; reading the UDC attribute
opens it read-only. mode_request no longer holds the daemon lock while
it sleeps between retries.

Tests: the transaction runs against the configfs fake with a fault
injected before every call, service calls included (errors, a
concurrent binder, a concurrent unlinker, the UDC vanishing):
afterwards the gadget is bound, every pin is linked, while it was
unbound only window calls ran, no service started before the window
ended, only the service of the mode the gadget ended up in was started
(a failed rung never starts the target's), and exactly that service
runs. Daemon tests cover startup adoption, host_mode, SIGTERM, a
failing mlockall, a host role excursion of an adopted and of a declared
gadget, and a stale state file. An ignored test adopts a gadget with a
non-FunctionFS pin (acm.pin) on dummy_hcd with memory really locked,
switches developer -> charging_only -> developer, and checks that
SIGTERM makes no configfs write and leaves the UDC state and the host
side's device number unchanged.

