-- **storage\_path** (String): path to the image to serve, for example: `/data/myimage.iso`
+- `set_mode` fails with `UDC <udc> is held by declared gadget <dir>; not
+  replacing it` while the gadget holds the UDC, and no USB role is written.
+- On shutdown the gadget is left untouched.
+- Switching to host role only removes gadgets usb-signaller created.
+- `host_mode` is refused while a declared gadget with pinned functions is bound.
+- While its UDC is away, for example during a switch to host role, the gadget
+  keeps its UDC attribute and the kernel binds it again when the UDC returns.
+  Meanwhile `set_mode` fails with `declared gadget <dir> waits for UDC <udc>
+  to return; not replacing it`, and no USB role is written that would bring
+  the UDC back early.
+
+With `adopt = true`, a declared gadget is **adopted** instead: usb-signaller
+manages its modes in place, next to the pinned functions. At startup, and
+before every mode switch, a bound gadget is adopted when:
+
+- its UDC attribute names the UDC it is bound to;
+- it has one configuration, or `config` names one;
+- it has at least one pinned function, and every pin exists, is linked in that
+  configuration (link names may differ from function names) and, for a
+  FunctionFS pin, reads `ready` as 1. Kernels before 6.9 have no `ready`
+  attribute; a bound pin then counts as ready.
+
+Otherwise it stays declared, and usb-signaller logs why it is not adopted.
+Adopting does not touch the gadget or the bus.
+
+The mode of an adopted gadget is read from the functions linked next to the
+pins: none is `charging_only`, `ncm.usb0` is `developer_mode` (or
+`tethering_mode`, if that was the last mode set). While any other function
+that is neither pinned nor one of usb-signaller's own (`ncm.usb0`, `ffs.mtp`,
+`ffs.accessory`, `mass_storage.0`) is linked, mode switches are refused.
+
+A mode switch on an adopted gadget:
+
+1. **Prepare**, while the gadget is still bound: create the new function
+   directories and stop the old mode's service.
+2. **Unbind, relink, bind**, on a dedicated thread that only makes system
+   calls on files opened beforehand. Pins are never unlinked. If the new
+   composition does not bind, the previous one is restored, and if that fails
+   too only the pins are linked, retried until the gadget is bound again.
+3. **Finish**, bound again: remove the functions that left and start the new
+   mode's service.
+
+If the functions do not change, for example between `developer_mode` and
+`tethering_mode`, or when the initramfs already linked `ncm.usb0` for a
+`developer_mode` default, nothing in configfs is written and the gadget stays
+bound: only the services change. Pinned functions, the gadget, its
+configurations and its identity are never removed or written.
+
+While a gadget is adopted:
+
+- Only `charging_only`, `developer_mode` and `tethering_mode` are supported.
+  Other modes, including `host_mode`, fail with `<mode> is not supported while
+  gadget <dir> is adopted`, and nothing is touched.
+- No USB role is written. While the gadget's UDC is away (host role), it stays
+  adopted and mode switches fail with `adopted gadget <dir> waits for UDC
+  <udc> to return; not switching modes` until the kernel has bound it again.
+- Stopping or restarting usb-signaller writes nothing to the gadget, also
+  while its UDC is away, and leaves the mode's service running; the next
+  instance adopts it again.
+- usb-signaller locks its memory (`mlockall`) for the rest of its life, so it
+  never waits for the root filesystem while the gadget is unbound. If memory
+  cannot be locked, the gadget is not adopted, only protected.
+- `/run/usb-signaller/state.env` names the gadget, configuration, mode, NCM
+  interface and connection name. The developer and tethering scripts read it
+  to find the interface; it is removed before usb-signaller switches one of
+  its own gadgets.
+
+Future work: MTP, mass storage and CD-ROM on adopted gadgets, writing the
+identity when `keep_identity = false`, a watchdog that binds an adopted gadget
+again after an unexpected unbind, and adopting a gadget that is bound after
+usb-signaller started (today it is adopted at the next mode switch).
 
